programming languages are divided into ---> low level , high level [more user  friendly]
1.low level -->  Machine  language --> which can directly run on cpu  [ 0  or 1  ] -->not portable
                 Assembly language ---> 0 , 1 is replaced by instructions --> specific to achitecture and sometime os eg.. 8051 / intel
2.high level --> procedural , object oriented [ portable ]
                

procedural programming  ---> converting program into much smaller programs called procedures / functions








 ---> scope of variables :- local var , global var
      Note: redefnition of global  variable inside a function is allowed
----> variable modifiers -> auto , extern
      auto : an automatic variable is variable which automatically get derstoyed after the completion of the function or it's scope
 means memory allocated to it will be de-allocated to other var.
by default all vars inside a function are automatic vars no need to specify the auto keyword
eg 1.

#include<stdio.h>                               
int main(){
auto int var;
printf("%d",var);
return 0;
}

op : any value ( garbage )
____________________________________________

eg 2.

#include<stdio.h>
auto int var;    //global
int main(){
printf("%d",var);
return 0;
}

op :0 ---> the global vars are automatically initialised to 0
